=== R4/T4 DIFFERENTIAL OSCILLATOR: A CONSISTENT READING WHOSE DIVIDE RATIO IS NOT YET VALIDATED ===
[STATUS UPDATE 2026-11-23, appended header note, body below unchanged: the divide
 ratio was validated THE NEXT DAY by a cell-identical control design on silicon
 (divide_ratio_control.log, ratio 256), and the reading was reproduced in a third
 independent session (remeasure_2026-11-23/, pooled mean 397.62 MHz over 3
 sessions, published PROVISIONAL). The header below predates both.]
Date: 2026-11-22 ~13:00-13:50 UTC+7
Host: macOS, Digilent HS2 (FT232H 0x0403:0x6014), busdev 1:3
Bitstream: clock_probe_r4t4.bit  md5 56a2e4482c855a1be68bb9daeef837a4
Netlist:  clock_probe_r4t4.netlist.json.gz (the ground truth for this bitstream)

WHAT WAS ATTEMPTED, in order, and what each attempt showed:

1. An 8-level flip-flop RIPPLE prescaler (md5 933f4c08...).
   Result: the counter read FROZEN - 0 on every read.
   Diagnosis: an 8-level XOR/AND feedback chain does not settle inside 200 MHz's 5 ns cycle.
   The board's clock is fine; the PROBE was too slow.

2. A CARRY-CHAIN prescaler.
   Result: would not route in 5 seeds - openXC7 split the CARRY4 chain across non-adjacent
   slices (the same defect that blocks the attention design).

3. A 6-level ripple prescaler (md5 a9d6f866...).
   Result: the counter advanced, but ERRATICALLY - deltas of 1,003,291, 1,041,431, 86,413,
   1,679, 37,527 counts in identical windows. A ripple that only sometimes settles.

4. A TWO-STAGE JOHNSON SHIFT REGISTER prescaler, tick taken on its RISING EDGE
   (md5 56a2e448..., the bitstream measured here).
   The Johnson counter's feedback is ONE level deep, so it runs at any clock.
   Result: CONSISTENT.

