--- WHY THIS IS NOT PUBLISHED AS A CLOCK MEASUREMENT ---

The number above is 398.69 MHz ASSUMING the two-stage Johnson chain divides by 256. That
assumption is the whole measurement, and it has NOT been validated independently:

  - The CFGMCLK CONTROL with the identical Johnson chain would not route in 40 seeds. The
    placer scattered the BSCANE2 shift register every time. That control is the whole point:
    CFGMCLK is independently measured at 67.16 MHz, so if the Johnson chain divides by 256
    the control must read ~67 MHz.
  - An independent divide path was tried: a two-stage RIPPLE prescaler (4-bit then 8-bit,
    /4096). It read FROZEN at 0 on 12/12 reads, because ripples do not settle at 200 MHz.
  - A 6-bit ripple variant was also frozen. Ripples are simply not usable at 200 MHz here.

If the chain actually divides by 512, the same reading is 199.3 MHz - which would match the
parent repository's documented "200 MHz oscillator on R4/T4" EXACTLY. That possibility has
not been excluded, and a factor of two is exactly the kind of error this portfolio's own
standards forbid publishing.

STATUS: NOT PUBLISHED. Recorded as a consistent reading with an unvalidated divide ratio.
The next step is to get the CFGMCLK+Johnson control to route (the netlist is on the GPU box at
/tmp/clock_probe_johnson_cfg.json) and read it; that single measurement settles the factor.

WHAT THIS DOES ESTABLISH, WITHOUT THE DIVIDE RATIO:
  - R4/T4 carries a RUNNING, STABLE clock. The counter advances monotonically with a 1.9%
    spread and a valid magic on every read. A dead or floating pin cannot do that.
  - The board's clock is usable for a real design, unlike the fabric ring oscillator.
  - A measurement probe on this board needs a prescaler whose feedback settles in 5 ns:
    a JOHNSON shift register (one level) works, a RIPPLE does not.
